    "created_at": "2019-05-23T13:02:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-05-23T13:02:34Z",
    "body": "Ex: https://bokand.github.io/bugs/fragment-scrolling.html\r\n\r\nClick the first button to navigate a same-origin, offscreen, iframe to a page with a fragment.\r\n\r\n**Chrome, Safari, Edge(18)**: Scroll the top document so that the iframe comes into view\r\n**Firefox**: Does not scroll the top document\r\n\r\nClick the second button to navigate to a #top fragment this time:\r\n\r\n**Chrome, Safari**: Scroll the top document so that the iframe comes into view\r\n**Firefox,Edge(18)**: Does not scroll the top document\r\n\r\nNote: if the iframe document is cross-origin, all browsers prevent scrolling the top document.\r\n\r\nCertainly #top and other fragments should be consistent.\r\n\r\nIn general, Firefox's behavior seems more intuitive to me - loading a URL with a fragment in an iframe means the document should load with the fragment visible, not necessarily that we should bring it to the users attention.\r\n\r\n`scrollIntoView()` is a bit more debatable, at the least there might be compat-rist there.\r\n\r\nWould anyone object to changing the spec text to clarify that the scroll into view steps for a fragment scroll shouldn't cross frame boundaries? (i.e. we should codify Firefox's behavior)\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
